WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.360
Hello, Internet.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.990
Welcome to Film Theory.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.720
The show that promises to let you sit and
watch public domain cartoons for as long as

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.470
you want, provided you stab that subscribe
button right in the eye.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.160
Now, I'm not a man with a whole lot of free
time.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.149
Between launching new channels (subscribe
to Style Theory, by the way), writing and

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:59.270
rewriting FNAF timelines, being a father and,
you know, just trying to have an actual life,

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.790
things can get pretty darn busy.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:04.410
But in what little free time I've had lately,
I've been scrolling through the TikToks to

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.290
see what's hip with the kiddos, and what I've
seen

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.220
has been nothing but pages and pages of Pedro
Pascal fan cams.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.440
Do not make fan cams of school staff

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.320
Oh, you can't stop me, Pedro.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.180
I can and I will.

00:01:27.578 --> 00:01:32.216
But once I scrolled past those, I was inundated
by something far less esthetic and much more scary.

00:01:32.216 --> 00:01:36.729
A terrifying new horror movie called Skinamarink,
Everything I saw heralded this thing as the

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.510
scariest movie EVAR

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.829
And based on the trailer alone, I was hooked.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.630
Ladies and gentlemen, we have reached peak
analog horror.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.460
So intrigued and a little bit scared.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.399
I did what any normal person would do.

00:02:00.399 --> 00:02:04.479
I sat down at 9 a.m. on a Wednesday morning
with two of my employees to watch through

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:05.479
this puppy.

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:07.520
And boy, was it controversial.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.780
One of them rated it an eight.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.740
The other rated it a zero and said it didn't
even deserve to be called a movie.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.879
And honestly, I can't really blame him for
his opinion.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:19.580
5 minutes in and it's immediately clear why
this thing is going to be a divisive movie.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.060
It's about 100 minutes of staring at dark
walls and grainy hallways while people whisper

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.099
in the background.

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:26.930
It is not for everyone.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.489
As for my opinion, I can tell you that it
was probably the most active movie watching

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:34.629
experience that I've ever had, with my eyes
constantly scanning the frame, questioning

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:38.250
whether what I was seeing was a shadow monster
or just artifacting on the camera.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.110
I wasn't just passively watching a movie.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:45.289
I was having to solve each and every frame,
trying to understand what exactly I was looking at.

00:02:45.289 --> 00:02:46.989
TL;DR It worked for me.

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:48.250
I really enjoyed it.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:53.771
But not only is Skinamarink a visually challenging
movie, it also is a narratively challenging one.

00:02:53.771 --> 00:02:57.430
The story, as it's presented to us, is about
two young children, Kaylee and Kevin, who

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.600
wake up in the middle of the night to find
that their parents have disappeared, along

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.349
with all the house's doors and windows.

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:06.971
And so the kids in the movie respond like
any normal child would do in this sort of situation.

00:03:06.971 --> 00:03:10.900
They park themselves right in front of that
TV and start binge watching some old school

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.870
copyright neutral cartoons.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:16.670
Pretty soon, though, things get worse as they
begin to realize that they're not alone inside

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.051
the house as a formless demon who makes the
kids do horrific things like

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:22.922
Walk up the stairs and

00:03:24.146 --> 00:03:25.549
Walk up the stairs again.

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:27.790
He is a very cardiovascular ghost.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:28.290
Oh, yeah.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.562
Also put a knife in your eye.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.442
We don't actually see anything, but the sudden
switch to implied violence is just incredibly jarring.

00:03:36.442 --> 00:03:39.340
Bit by bit, things get progressively worse
for our young duo.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.100
Toys begin sticking to the ceiling.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.230
Night lights get unplugged.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:43.620
The toilets disappear.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.260
Man, this demon is ruthless.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.130
The eye thing, yeah, that was brutal.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.330
But making them poop in buckets?

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:49.620
That’s just cruel.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.450
Eventually, Kaylee disappears, leaving the
four year old Kevin alone in a house that

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.319
has literally and figuratively turned upside
down.

00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:01.569
As the movie ends, we see this image stating
572 days, which is super random, because up

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:04.990
to this point, there really hasn't been any
clear focus on the passage of time.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:09.010
And then you have this brutally long pull
out shot that lasts almost two consecutive

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.410
minutes, just really milking it for that watch
time, aren't they?

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.930
On an unrelated note, did I mention the director
for this thing got his start on YouTube?

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:17.930
I See you.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:18.930
I know what you're doing.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.040
I know your secrets.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.700
There's a bit more screaming and whispering
and blood before the movie ultimately ends,

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.862
with a blurry figure fading into frame, telling
Kevin to.

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:29.419
And that's about it.

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:30.419
Clear as crystal, right?

00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:31.419
Well, yeah, actually, kinda.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:34.949
On the surface, it seems like just another
spooky ghost movie.

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:39.209
But looking at it that way, actually leaves
a lot of unanswered questions: What was the monster?

00:04:39.209 --> 00:04:40.810
Why did everything disappear?

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.040
Where did the parents go?

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.600
What is the significance of 572 days?

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.610
And what does that ending actually mean?

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:49.710
In short, this movie is an open invite for
theorists to solve.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:50.710
And you know what?

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:51.710
I think I cracked it.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.440
What seems like a simple ghost story is actually
a tragedy.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.030
A terribly sad story about loss, grief and
family trauma.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.630
All told from the perspective of a four year
old child.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.560
Everything we see in the film feels like a
paranormal situation, right?

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.560
We hear voices.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.690
We see things stuck to the ceiling.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:09.540
We have doors disappearing, and we have this
constant grain and distortion.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.810
What could cause all of this, if not a demon?

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.730
Well, let's just start from the very beginning.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:17.730
The movie kicks off with a seemingly random
event where Kevin falls down some stairs.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.380
In the middle of the night, we see a series
of clips of Kevin stealing blankets from his

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:22.910
closet, sitting up on top of the stairs.

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:27.369
And then we get an upside down shot of the
bottom of the stairs after hearing a loud thud.

00:05:27.369 --> 00:05:28.692
This is then followed up by his dad on the
phone.

00:05:33.569 --> 00:05:34.770
It seems pretty cut and dry.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.560
Kevin falls down some stairs and hits his
head.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.260
There's just one problem with that.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.000
This incident is never mentioned again.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.069
It is completely arbitrary for everything
that comes afterwards.

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:45.360
It goes nowhere and nothing comes of it.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.080
Why would the director be showing us this
then?

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.000
Why is it the first event of the movie?

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.714
Well, I suspect it's because this is the real
incident that incites everything we see happen afterward.

00:05:54.714 --> 00:05:59.240
The fall sends Kevin into a coma, and everything
we see from that point onward in the movie

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.810
are the real world events being filtered and
processed through his unconscious condition.

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:07.710
Now, let me immediately say that I know how
lame coma theories are.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:12.460
You can make anything fit into a coma or dream
theory because there's no logic in these things.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.430
Anything can be bent to be a piece of evidence.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.720
There is a reason that across the 1,012 theories
that I've written across the last decade,

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.020
only one of them has claimed that everything
was a dream.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:26.150
That's because I only pull them out when I
think that that's the author's true intent.

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:29.819
And with Skinamarink, I am convinced that
this is the right interpretation.

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:34.090
Obviously, we just talked about the inciting
incident of a head injury, but there is so,

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:35.400
so much more here.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.110
Across the entirety of the movie there are
three random details of this supposed haunting

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:43.229
that stood out to me as unusual: the missing
toilets, the request to stick a knife in Kevin's

00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:46.240
eye, and the marker of 572 days.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.380
So let's just go back and tackle these one
at a time.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:50.860
First, let's just take a look at the basic
concept of the film itself.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.280
While the idea of doors and windows disappearing
from a house makes for a creepy paranormal

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.130
setup, what doesn't fit quite as well as the
disappearance of the toilets.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.500
The lack of doors and windows I totally get.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.730
You're trapped inside of a situation that
you can't escape from.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.340
You're at the mercy of the demon.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.340
But the toilets?

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.930
I mean, why would a paranormal entity do that?

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:13.250
It's either a big troll or this isn't actually
a ghost that we're dealing with.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.600
So let's just assume that everything after
Kevin falls down the stairs and hits his head

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.050
takes place in a hospital.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:22.958
Well, if that's truly the case, then the disappearing
toilets would be the reality of his current situation.

00:07:22.958 --> 00:07:27.750
He's no longer physically going to the bathroom
and toilets anymore, depending on the policy

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:32.129
of the hospital he's in, he's likely using
a bedpan, diapers or a rectal catheter to

00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:33.280
excrete his waste.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.384
This would also explain the disappearing doors
and windows that form the core of this movie's plot.

00:07:37.384 --> 00:07:42.380
On one hand, it's symbolic of Kevin now being
trapped inside his own mind with no escape.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:46.210
That's why near the end of the movie, when
he asks to watch something happy, we see visions

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.210
of a door.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:51.300
It's a moment or a chance of escape, but the
disappearing doors and windows could also

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.509
literally be the hospital room around him,
a claustrophobic space that's blocked off

00:07:55.509 --> 00:07:56.509
by curtains.

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:58.919
In fact, look closer at your typical hospital
room.

00:07:58.919 --> 00:07:59.919
And what do you see in each one?

00:07:59.919 --> 00:08:03.680
A TV sitting in front of the patient, playing
all day, every day.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:04.680
And what do we see in Skinamarink?

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.470
A TV as a constant presence throughout everything
in the film.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:12.470
So much of this movie is spent sitting in
front of a TV, specifically public domain

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:13.470
cartoons.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.090
It's got to mean something, right?

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:16.090
It does.

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:18.919
These cartoons are the entire plot of the
movie.

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:22.300
If you take a closer look at the cartoons
that play and when they play, they seem to

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.050
be telling us the real story of what's happening
in the movie.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.960
One of the first cartoons we see after Kevin's
accident is this, a baby bird being shot out

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.669
of the sky and appearing dead?

00:08:30.669 --> 00:08:33.810
This cartoon is the song of the Birds from
1935.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:36.779
Except there's a twist here if you watch beyond
what they show in Skinamarink.

00:08:36.779 --> 00:08:40.979
When the birds go to bury the baby's body,
he wakes up and everyone celebrates.

00:08:40.979 --> 00:08:44.500
This cartoon, I suspect, is representative
of Kevin's injury.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.750
He's the baby bird who was knocked unconscious.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:48.821
This clip even plays after the kids ask the
question.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:55.550
It's because Mom thinks that her baby bird
is dead or maybe just close to death.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:09:00.770
The second major cartoon we see is the Cobweb
Hotel from 1936, where two newlywed flies

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:05.390
watch helplessly as a young fly is trapped
inside a monstrous spider's bed of webs.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.360
They then have to fight to escape from the
spider.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.200
Now, this could very easily be symbolic of
Kevin and Kaylee fighting against the monster

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:12.540
that has them trapped inside the house.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:17.620
But it could also be Kevin's parents forced
to watch their son trapped inside of a hospital

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.610
bed, unable to escape.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:23.060
Considering that the two newly wed flies are
adults, I suspect it's the second explanation.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:27.180
The third major cartoon reference we get is
to 1931's Bimbo's initiation.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.650
This one we don't actually see, but I was
able to recognize the audio from research

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.954
we did years ago when we did our Bendy and
the Ink Machine episode about rubber hose animation.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:44.000
In this one, Bimbo falls down a sewer only
to be locked in a prison of inescapable false

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.151
doors, all while the world turns upside down
around him.

00:09:47.151 --> 00:09:51.959
Ha. A character falls down, winds up trapped in
a place without doors and everything turns

00:09:51.959 --> 00:09:52.959
upside down.

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:54.440
Why even have a movie Skinamarink?

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:56.740
You told us everything we needed to know,
right there.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.661
And in the final cartoon, 1939’s Prest-O
Change-O.

00:09:59.661 --> 00:10:03.000
We see this clip of a dog that's being shot
in the eye.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:03.865
Again, sound familiar?

00:10:06.339 --> 00:10:08.620
The cartoons are telling the story of Kevin's
life.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:10.050
And why are they always on?

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:11.140
Why are they always playing?

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.220
Because he's sitting in a hospital room, stuck
in front of a TV.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.970
But With all that said, let's look a bit closer
at the eye bit.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:20.500
It's one of the very few moments of implied
violence in the movie so obviously it's going

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:21.570
to stand out as important.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:22.990
But again, why?

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:24.550
Why this of all things?

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:25.590
just to shock us?

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:26.910
Again, I don't think so.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:30.514
Let's take a look back at what caused Kevin
to fall down the stairs in the first place.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:34.890
Hayley says he was sleepwalking.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.560
Sleepwalking, hallucinations, eye pain.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.740
it doesn't take Derek Shepherd to know what's
going on with this one.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.120
Kevin has a brain tumor.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:46.089
And, yeah, that was a Gray's Anatomy reference
for any of you under the age of 45.

00:10:46.089 --> 00:10:50.290
I suspect Kevin was suffering from a tumor
on the frontal lobe of his brain, thereby

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:53.730
causing him to sleepwalk, hallucinate and
ultimately fall down the stairs.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:58.570
In fact, brain tumors are the most common
form of solid tumor inside of children, affecting

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:00.790
about 5,000 in the U.S. every year.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:05.491
What I suspect happened here was that the
brain tumor caused his sleepwalking and subsequent fall.

00:11:05.491 --> 00:11:08.820
And while the fall itself might have resulted
in a minor injury, considering what we hear

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:12.620
from Dad's phone call, the subsequent hospital
visit, or the damage that the fall caused

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.250
made the tumor issue worse.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:17.220
With kevin ultimately winding up in a coma
shortly thereafter.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.731
So what does all of that have to do with knives
and eyes?

00:11:19.731 --> 00:11:23.139
Well, I don't think that it was just any knife
we're talking about here.

00:11:23.139 --> 00:11:26.420
I think what's going on is a surgical procedure.

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:29.620
One way to remove tumors from the frontal
lobe of the brain is to perform a procedure

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:34.899
known as a supraorbital keyhole craniotomy,
a type of brain surgery where they go in through,

00:11:34.899 --> 00:11:36.480
you guessed it, the eye.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:39.930
It's minimally invasive and it's an easier
way to reach the frontal lobe to remove the

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:43.850
tumor, which is why Kevin feels like he's
being stabbed in the eye with a knife.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:47.370
This would also explain another weird detail
that we see towards the end of the film.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.920
At the top of the movie, we get a montage
of various family photos hanging on the wall.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:55.130
At the end, we get a similar montage, but
this time everyone is missing their head or

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:57.170
the faces have been weirdly distorted.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:01.870
This would be the result of a condition known
as prosopagnosia, also known as face blindness,

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.899
where you cannot recognize people's faces.

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:07.590
It's a symptom that often appears in patients
with brain tumors.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:11.300
This leads us then to the ending of the film
where we see blood being sprayed on the floor

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:15.279
over and over, all while we hear screaming
in the background and the repeating music

00:12:15.279 --> 00:12:18.730
from the Prest-O Change-O cartoon, the one
where the dog gets shot in the eye.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:22.639
If you turn on the closed captions, the first
round of screaming we hear is captioned as

00:12:22.639 --> 00:12:23.850
“child crying”.

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:26.370
Thank you closed caption people (*o*) for
not naming him the crying child.

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.110
I just can't rewrite the damn FNAF timeline
again.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:33.370
The next round of screaming though, is credited
as “older child cries”.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:38.880
This scene, I suspect, is Kevin being operated
on over and over, over a prolonged period.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.220
That's why the blood spills on the carpet
repeatedly, why the music is pulled from the

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:47.152
cartoon that references his eye pain and why
the subtitles differentiate child and older child.

00:12:47.152 --> 00:12:52.700
It is two years of Kevin in a coma undergoing
procedures like this as doctors try and repeatedly

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:55.760
fail at removing the tumor that's endangering
his life.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.789
How do I know it's been two years?

00:12:57.789 --> 00:13:01.220
the 572 days marker that we see in the film's
final act.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:04.890
At no point in the movie is there ever any
